# Tailwind v4 — CSS variables in `className`

This project uses **Tailwind CSS v4**. For theme tokens defined as CSS custom properties (for example `--na-cyan`, `--na-border`), use the **v4 variable shorthand** on utilities. Do not use the older verbose arbitrary forms.

## Required

- **Text / border / background colors from variables**  
  Use `utility-(--var-name)`:
  - `text-(--na-cyan)` not `text-[color:var(--na-cyan)]`
  - `border-(--na-border)` not `border-[color:var(--na-border)]`
  - `bg-(--na-surface)` not `bg-[color:var(--na-surface)]`

- **Box shadow from a variable**
  - `shadow-(--na-glow-grid)` not `shadow-[var(--na-glow-grid)]`

## Also prefer

- Integer z-index utilities when valid: `z-1` not `z-[1]` (when the linter suggests the simpler form).

## Why

The verbose `[color:var(...)]` / `[var(...)]` forms trigger Tailwind IntelliSense warnings and are redundant in v4; the shorthand is shorter and is the supported style for “pass a CSS variable into this utility.”

When adding or editing `className` strings in TSX/JSX, default to the shorthand for any `--na-*` (or other project) CSS variables.
